## Story Description
Implement the supplier master table builder in src/suppliers/harmoniser.py (partial implementation — the master builder portion). This takes a DataFrame of unique raw supplier names and builds the canonical supplier_master table by deduplicating against any existing entries.

## Acceptance Criteria
- Edit file: src/suppliers/harmoniser.py — implement build_supplier_master(raw_suppliers: pd.DataFrame, engine, config) -> pd.DataFrame function (not a class yet — just this function)
- raw_suppliers has columns: raw_supplier_name, raw_supplier_id (may be null), normalised_name (pre-computed)
- For each unique normalised_name: generate canonical_supplier_id as first 12 chars of SHA256(normalised_name.encode()).hexdigest()
- Build canonical_name as the most-common raw_supplier_name among all rows sharing the same normalised_name (title-cased)
- Check if canonical_supplier_id already exists in supplier_master table (get_transactions equivalent for supplier_master). If exists, skip insert (idempotent).
- Insert new canonical suppliers into supplier_master_table with: canonical_supplier_id, canonical_name, parent_company_id=None, parent_name=None, country=None, identifiers=JSON of {raw_ids: [list of unique raw_supplier_ids]}, created_at=now, updated_at=now
- Return DataFrame of all canonical suppliers (both new and existing) with columns: canonical_supplier_id, canonical_name, normalised_name
- Verify: given the 10-row sample data, the 5 Acme/ACME/acme rows produce exactly 1 canonical supplier entry, and the 2 Sodexo rows produce exactly 1 canonical supplier entry

